{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019S7n6somKDu3v9p9eQu9bQ","type":"tool_result","content":"    pooled = np.concatenate([yhat, yhat_unl])\n    edges = np.unique(np.quantile(pooled, np.linspace(0, 1, n_bins + 1)[1:-1]))\n    sl = np.searchsorted(edges, yhat, side=\"right\")\n    su = np.searchsorted(edges, yhat_unl, side=\"right\")\n    center, var = 0.0, 0.0\n    for b in range(edges.size + 1):\n        ml, mu_ = sl == b, su == b\n        nb, nu = int(ml.sum()), int(mu_.sum())\n        if nu == 0:\n            continue\n        if nb < 2:\n            return classical_ci(y, alpha)\n        w = nu / yhat_unl.size\n        rb = y[ml] - yhat[ml]\n        center += w * (yhat_unl[mu_].mean() + rb.mean())\n        var += w * w * (rb.var(ddof=1) / nb + (yhat_unl[mu_].var(ddof=1) / nu if nu > 1 else 0.0))\n    half = NormalDist().inv_cdf(1.0 - alpha / 2.0) * math.sqrt(var)\n    return center - half, center + half\n\n\ndef _best_interval(y, yhat, yhat_unl, alpha, seed):\n    \"\"\"PPI++ baseline versus cross-fitted recalibrated candidates; a candidate replaces the\n    baseline only if its half-width is at least 1% smaller (guards against selection noise).\"\"\"\n    base = ppi_plus_plus_mean_ci(y, yhat, yhat_unl, alpha)\n    best, best_half = base, 0.5 * (base[1] - base[0])\n    if not (math.isfinite(base[0]) and math.isfinite(base[1])):\n        best, best_half = classical_ci(y, alpha), math.inf\n    threshold = 0.99 * best_half\n    for method in (\"isotonic\", \"binned\"):\n        try:\n            cand = crppi_mean_ci(y, yhat, yhat_unl, alpha, method=method, seed=seed)\n        except Exception:\n            cand = None\n        if cand is None or not (math.isfinite(cand[0]) and math.isfinite(cand[1])):\n            continue\n        half = 0.5 * (cand[1] - cand[0])\n        if half < threshold:\n            best, threshold = cand, half\n    return best\n\n\ndef _estimate(request):\n    n, big_n, alpha = request[\"n\"], request[\"N\"], request[\"alpha\"]\n    y = _decode(request[\"Y_labeled\"], \"Y_labeled\")\n    yhat = _decode(request[\"Yhat_labeled\"], \"Yhat_labeled\")\n    yhat_unl = _decode(request[\"Yhat_unlabeled\"], \"Yhat_unlabeled\")\n    if y.size != n or yhat.size != n or yhat_unl.size != big_n or not 0.0 < alpha < 1.0:\n        raise ValueError(\"inconsistent estimate request\")\n    seed = int(_STATE[\"rng\"].integers(0, 2 ** 31 - 1))\n    lo, hi = _best_interval(y, yhat, yhat_unl, alpha, seed)\n    if not (math.isfinite(lo) and math.isfinite(hi)):\n        lo, hi = classical_ci(y, alpha)\n    return {\"lo\": float(lo), \"hi\": float(hi)}\n\n\ndef handle(request):\n    op = request.get(\"op\") if isinstance(request, dict) else None\n    if op == \"reset\":\n        seed = request[\"seed\"]\n        if type(seed) is not int or seed < 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"bad seed\")\n40:Prediction-powered inference (PPI) combines a small labeled sample, a large unlabeled sample and a fixed pretrained predictor into a valid confidence interval for a population mean. We ask how much further such an interval can be shortened when labels, unlabeled data and predictor are all held fixed. Writing PPI++ as a control-variate estimator, we show that the attainable gain is governed by the gap between the squared correlation of $Y$ with $\\Yhat$ and the correlation ratio of $Y$ given $\\Yhat$, plus a clipping effect on the tuning weight. We evaluate a cross-fitted recalibration (isotonic or binned) added as a second control variate, with a fallback to PPI++, on a sealed evaluation of four real pools and known-truth synthetic regimes. The interval is 2.2\\% narrower than PPI++ (geometric mean over five families), 6.8\\% narrower than PPI with $\\lambda{=}1$ and 30\\% narrower than the labeled-only interval; per-family reductions range from 0.2\\% to 4.0\\%. Pooled coverage is 0.934 (lowest family 0.91), below the nominal 0.95 although not rejected by the evaluator's exact tests, and the observed narrowing exceeds our population-level ceiling in four of five families. The evidence supports a small, regime-dependent gain; it does not show that the gain is free of a coverage cost.\n48:Our route is a nested one. PPI++ is a control-variate estimator whose control variate is $\\Yhat$ with a scalar weight clipped to $[0,1]$. We add a second control variate $g(\\Yhat)$, a recalibration of the prediction fitted on the labeled data by cross-fitting, and let a variance-minimizing weight vector combine the two. A selection rule falls back to PPI++ unless the recalibrated interval is at least 1\\% shorter. The question then reduces to how much variance the second control variate can remove.\n53:\\item \\textbf{A recalibrated PPI interval} with isotonic and binned recalibrators, cross-fitting, Student-$t$ critical values and a 1\\%-margin fallback to PPI++ (Section~\\ref{sec:method}).\n61:\\caption{Setting and compared constructions. With labels, unlabeled records and the predictor fixed, three intervals are built from the same data: the labeled-only interval, PPI++ (scalar weight on $\\Yhat$), and recalibrated PPI (cross-fitted $g(\\Yhat)$ as an additional control variate, with a fallback to PPI++). Each is scored by empirical coverage and by the width ratio to PPI++; the achievable narrowing is bounded by the drop from $\\Var(Y-\\lambda\\Yhat)$ to the residual variance of the best $g$.}\n107:PPI++ & $\\Yhat$ & scalar, estimated, clipped to $[0,1]$ & normal; primary comparator, also the fallback \\\\\n109:Recalibrated PPI (studied) & $\\Yhat$ and cross-fitted $g(\\Yhat)$ & vector, estimated, unclipped & Student-$t$, $n{-}1{-}p$ d.f.; adopted only if $\\ge1\\%$ shorter than PPI++ \\\\\n117:\\paragraph{Selection with fallback.} The PPI++ interval is computed first. Each recalibrated candidate replaces the current best only if its half-width is below 0.99 times the current best; otherwise PPI++ is returned. If $n<20$ or $\\Yhat$ is constant, no candidate is formed. The 1\\% margin is a heuristic guard against selection noise, not a validity guarantee; the interval chosen by comparing estimated widths is data-dependent, and the standard-error estimate does not account for the estimation of $g$ or of $\\lambda$ beyond the degrees-of-freedom correction.\n176:Asymptotic variance reduction from recalibration cannot account for this part. We did not run a diagnostic that isolates its source. Candidate explanations, none tested here, are (a) finite-sample optimism of an interval whose weight and residual variance are estimated on the same labeled data, (b) selection of the smaller of three estimated widths, which favors downward noise in the estimated standard error, and (c) differences in finite-sample variance estimation between the evaluated implementation and the reference baseline. Explanations (a) and (b) would also lower coverage and are consistent with the coverage shortfall of Section~\\ref{sec:validity}, but the available artifacts do not allow us to establish that link. For the two census pools with near-zero ceilings, the excess is 0.0009 and 0.0014; with no per-episode widths in the artifacts, we cannot tell whether it is distinguishable from pairing noise.\n216:\\paragraph{What the evidence supports.} With fixed inputs, an interval narrower than PPI++ exists in the families we tested, but the average margin is about 2\\% in width and the margin depends strongly on the pool: close to zero for the two other census pools (insurance, education) and a few percent for census income and the synthetic mixture. These margins come from the whole evaluated configuration (cross-fitted recalibration, Student-$t$ quantile, variance estimator and PPI++ fallback), none of whose components was ablated, so we do not attribute them to any single component. The population-level diagnostic is of the same sign and roughly the same size as the observed margin for the synthetic mixture and, less closely, for census income, but it predicts none for the plankton pool (a two-valued predictor whose unclipped optimal weight lies inside $[0,1]$), where the observed margin is 3.5\\%, and it does not account for the observed gain in four of five families.\n220:\\paragraph{Limitations.} (1) Estimand: only the population mean at $\\alpha=0.05$. (2) Sample sizes: $n=500$ on real pools and $n\\in\\{200,500\\}$ on synthetic draws; no sweep over $n$ or $N$, although the theory predicts that small $n$ erodes the gain. (3) Single sealed run, no confidence intervals on width ratios, no per-episode widths or per-family coverage beyond the minimum in the artifacts, and no controlled ablation of the recalibrator, the second control variate, the Student-$t$ quantile or the selection rule. The mechanisms discussed above are therefore hypotheses. (4) The ceiling is a population-level asymptotic quantity computed with the evaluator's finite pools as the population; it is a diagnostic, not a bound on a finite draw. (5) The truth for real pools is the pool mean, with draws from the pool with replacement, so the evaluation checks inference for that finite population rather than for the original scientific population. (6) The evaluator's diagnostics for the candidate are marked as unverified. (7) The selection rule makes the interval data-dependent and the 1\\% margin has no theoretical guarantee. (8) Stratified PPI is implemented but has no reported metrics and is not compared numerically.\n222:\\paragraph{Experiments that would test the hypotheses.} The mechanisms above can be tested with controlled comparisons that we did not run: (i) PPI++ with the weight clip removed, to separate the clipping component from nonlinear calibration; (ii) the recalibrated interval with the fallback disabled and with a fixed choice of recalibrator, to separate selection effects from the second control variate; (iii) the Student-$t$ quantile replaced by the normal quantile; (iv) coverage and width of every baseline, computed on the same draws and reported per family; and (v) a grid over $n$ and $N$ on the real pools, where the theory predicts a gain that shrinks with $n$ (estimation cost of order $p/n$) and with $r$. Until these are available, the decomposition in Section~\\ref{sec:headroomcheck} should be read as a diagnostic of what is possible, not as an attribution of what the evaluated interval does.\n227:With labels, unlabeled data and predictor held fixed, the evaluated configuration (cross-fitted recalibration as a second control variate, Student-$t$ quantile and a PPI++ fallback) gave mean intervals 2.2\\% narrower than PPI++ on average in a sealed evaluation, with reductions between 0.2\\% and 4.0\\% by family, and 6.8\\% and 30\\% narrower than PPI with $\\lambda{=}1$ and the labeled-only interval. No component was ablated, so the gain is not attributed to recalibration. A closed-form population-level headroom diagnostic is small (aggregate 0.0129) and regime-dependent, and matches the observed margin closely only for the synthetic mixture and roughly for census income; it is zero for the plankton pool and does not account for the observed gain in four of five families. Coverage point estimates are 0.934 pooled and 0.91 for the lowest family, below the nominal 0.95, although the evaluator's tests did not reject. We read the result as conditional support for a small gain, not as evidence that PPI++ intervals can be shortened for free. The most useful next experiments are a coverage and width comparison of an unclipped PPI++ weight and each recalibration component in isolation, per-episode width and coverage reporting for all baselines, and a sweep over $n$.\n242:Settings of the evaluated interval: $K=5$ shuffled folds seeded from the candidate's random generator; isotonic fit with clipped out-of-bounds prediction; binned fit with $\\min(10,\\max(2,\\lfloor 0.8n/25\\rfloor))$ bins (10 for $n=500$, 6 for $n=200$); pooled standardization of features; ridge $10^{-6}\\,\\mathrm{tr}(A)/p$ on the weight system; Student-$t$ quantile via a Cornish--Fisher expansion with degrees of freedom floored at 3; PPI++ weight clipped to $[0,1]$; fallback margin 0.99. The evaluator enforces a 3\\,s per-estimate deadline on the evaluated estimator.\n244:\\paragraph{Contrast variants.} The label-free basis variant uses a continuous piecewise-linear basis of $\\Yhat$ with knots at pooled quantiles, the model size $p\\in\\{1,2,3,5,8\\}$ is chosen by leave-one-out PRESS with a 2\\% improvement margin, the standard error uses leverage-corrected leave-one-out residuals, and a guard keeps the width ratio against PPI++ above 0.85. The stacked variant fits seven calibrators (isotonic, three binned with 3, 5 and 8 bins, monotone and local-linear kernels, linear), combines them by non-negative least squares with 4 inner folds, repeats $K=5$ cross-fitting $R=5$ times under a time budget, and uses averaged-residual variance plus a split-dispersion term with $n-3$ degrees of freedom.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e640e0b5-aaee-4784-876d-5668ed034a92","uuid":"00f9b351-7567-4927-8bf4-ca0d0b80f9f0","timestamp":"2026-11-11T12:35:26.912Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"    pooled = np.concatenate([yhat, yhat_unl])\n    edges = np.unique(np.quantile(pooled, np.linspace(0, 1, n_bins + 1)[1:-1]))\n    sl = np.searchsorted(edges, yhat, side=\"right\")\n    su = np.searchsorted(edges, yhat_unl, side=\"right\")\n    center, var = 0.0, 0.0\n    for b in range(edges.size + 1):\n        ml, mu_ = sl == b, su == b\n        nb, nu = int(ml.sum()), int(mu_.sum())\n        if nu == 0:\n            continue\n        if nb < 2:\n            return classical_ci(y, alpha)\n        w = nu / yhat_unl.size\n        rb = y[ml] - yhat[ml]\n        center += w * (yhat_unl[mu_].mean() + rb.mean())\n        var += w * w * (rb.var(ddof=1) / nb + (yhat_unl[mu_].var(ddof=1) / nu if nu > 1 else 0.0))\n    half = NormalDist().inv_cdf(1.0 - alpha / 2.0) * math.sqrt(var)\n    return center - half, center + half\n\n\ndef _best_interval(y, yhat, yhat_unl, alpha, seed):\n    \"\"\"PPI++ baseline versus cross-fitted recalibrated candidates; a candidate replaces the\n    baseline only if its half-width is at least 1% smaller (guards against selection noise).\"\"\"\n    base = ppi_plus_plus_mean_ci(y, yhat, yhat_unl, alpha)\n    best, best_half = base, 0.5 * (base[1] - base[0])\n    if not (math.isfinite(base[0]) and math.isfinite(base[1])):\n        best, best_half = classical_ci(y, alpha), math.inf\n    threshold = 0.99 * best_half\n    for method in (\"isotonic\", \"binned\"):\n        try:\n            cand = crppi_mean_ci(y, yhat, yhat_unl, alpha, method=method, seed=seed)\n        except Exception:\n            cand = None\n        if cand is None or not (math.isfinite(cand[0]) and math.isfinite(cand[1])):\n            continue\n        half = 0.5 * (cand[1] - cand[0])\n        if half < threshold:\n            best, threshold = cand, half\n    return best\n\n\ndef _estimate(request):\n    n, big_n, alpha = request[\"n\"], request[\"N\"], request[\"alpha\"]\n    y = _decode(request[\"Y_labeled\"], \"Y_labeled\")\n    yhat = _decode(request[\"Yhat_labeled\"], \"Yhat_labeled\")\n    yhat_unl = _decode(request[\"Yhat_unlabeled\"], \"Yhat_unlabeled\")\n    if y.size != n or yhat.size != n or yhat_unl.size != big_n or not 0.0 < alpha < 1.0:\n        raise ValueError(\"inconsistent estimate request\")\n    seed = int(_STATE[\"rng\"].integers(0, 2 ** 31 - 1))\n    lo, hi = _best_interval(y, yhat, yhat_unl, alpha, seed)\n    if not (math.isfinite(lo) and math.isfinite(hi)):\n        lo, hi = classical_ci(y, alpha)\n    return {\"lo\": float(lo), \"hi\": float(hi)}\n\n\ndef handle(request):\n    op = request.get(\"op\") if isinstance(request, dict) else None\n    if op == \"reset\":\n        seed = request[\"seed\"]\n        if type(seed) is not int or seed < 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"bad seed\")\n40:Prediction-powered inference (PPI) combines a small labeled sample, a large unlabeled sample and a fixed pretrained predictor into a valid confidence interval for a population mean. We ask how much further such an interval can be shortened when labels, unlabeled data and predictor are all held fixed. Writing PPI++ as a control-variate estimator, we show that the attainable gain is governed by the gap between the squared correlation of $Y$ with $\\Yhat$ and the correlation ratio of $Y$ given $\\Yhat$, plus a clipping effect on the tuning weight. We evaluate a cross-fitted recalibration (isotonic or binned) added as a second control variate, with a fallback to PPI++, on a sealed evaluation of four real pools and known-truth synthetic regimes. The interval is 2.2\\% narrower than PPI++ (geometric mean over five families), 6.8\\% narrower than PPI with $\\lambda{=}1$ and 30\\% narrower than the labeled-only interval; per-family reductions range from 0.2\\% to 4.0\\%. Pooled coverage is 0.934 (lowest family 0.91), below the nominal 0.95 although not rejected by the evaluator's exact tests, and the observed narrowing exceeds our population-level ceiling in four of five families. The evidence supports a small, regime-dependent gain; it does not show that the gain is free of a coverage cost.\n48:Our route is a nested one. PPI++ is a control-variate estimator whose control variate is $\\Yhat$ with a scalar weight clipped to $[0,1]$. We add a second control variate $g(\\Yhat)$, a recalibration of the prediction fitted on the labeled data by cross-fitting, and let a variance-minimizing weight vector combine the two. A selection rule falls back to PPI++ unless the recalibrated interval is at least 1\\% shorter. The question then reduces to how much variance the second control variate can remove.\n53:\\item \\textbf{A recalibrated PPI interval} with isotonic and binned recalibrators, cross-fitting, Student-$t$ critical values and a 1\\%-margin fallback to PPI++ (Section~\\ref{sec:method}).\n61:\\caption{Setting and compared constructions. With labels, unlabeled records and the predictor fixed, three intervals are built from the same data: the labeled-only interval, PPI++ (scalar weight on $\\Yhat$), and recalibrated PPI (cross-fitted $g(\\Yhat)$ as an additional control variate, with a fallback to PPI++). Each is scored by empirical coverage and by the width ratio to PPI++; the achievable narrowing is bounded by the drop from $\\Var(Y-\\lambda\\Yhat)$ to the residual variance of the best $g$.}\n107:PPI++ & $\\Yhat$ & scalar, estimated, clipped to $[0,1]$ & normal; primary comparator, also the fallback \\\\\n109:Recalibrated PPI (studied) & $\\Yhat$ and cross-fitted $g(\\Yhat)$ & vector, estimated, unclipped & Student-$t$, $n{-}1{-}p$ d.f.; adopted only if $\\ge1\\%$ shorter than PPI++ \\\\\n117:\\paragraph{Selection with fallback.} The PPI++ interval is computed first. Each recalibrated candidate replaces the current best only if its half-width is below 0.99 times the current best; otherwise PPI++ is returned. If $n<20$ or $\\Yhat$ is constant, no candidate is formed. The 1\\% margin is a heuristic guard against selection noise, not a validity guarantee; the interval chosen by comparing estimated widths is data-dependent, and the standard-error estimate does not account for the estimation of $g$ or of $\\lambda$ beyond the degrees-of-freedom correction.\n176:Asymptotic variance reduction from recalibration cannot account for this part. We did not run a diagnostic that isolates its source. Candidate explanations, none tested here, are (a) finite-sample optimism of an interval whose weight and residual variance are estimated on the same labeled data, (b) selection of the smaller of three estimated widths, which favors downward noise in the estimated standard error, and (c) differences in finite-sample variance estimation between the evaluated implementation and the reference baseline. Explanations (a) and (b) would also lower coverage and are consistent with the coverage shortfall of Section~\\ref{sec:validity}, but the available artifacts do not allow us to establish that link. For the two census pools with near-zero ceilings, the excess is 0.0009 and 0.0014; with no per-episode widths in the artifacts, we cannot tell whether it is distinguishable from pairing noise.\n216:\\paragraph{What the evidence supports.} With fixed inputs, an interval narrower than PPI++ exists in the families we tested, but the average margin is about 2\\% in width and the margin depends strongly on the pool: close to zero for the two other census pools (insurance, education) and a few percent for census income and the synthetic mixture. These margins come from the whole evaluated configuration (cross-fitted recalibration, Student-$t$ quantile, variance estimator and PPI++ fallback), none of whose components was ablated, so we do not attribute them to any single component. The population-level diagnostic is of the same sign and roughly the same size as the observed margin for the synthetic mixture and, less closely, for census income, but it predicts none for the plankton pool (a two-valued predictor whose unclipped optimal weight lies inside $[0,1]$), where the observed margin is 3.5\\%, and it does not account for the observed gain in four of five families.\n220:\\paragraph{Limitations.} (1) Estimand: only the population mean at $\\alpha=0.05$. (2) Sample sizes: $n=500$ on real pools and $n\\in\\{200,500\\}$ on synthetic draws; no sweep over $n$ or $N$, although the theory predicts that small $n$ erodes the gain. (3) Single sealed run, no confidence intervals on width ratios, no per-episode widths or per-family coverage beyond the minimum in the artifacts, and no controlled ablation of the recalibrator, the second control variate, the Student-$t$ quantile or the selection rule. The mechanisms discussed above are therefore hypotheses. (4) The ceiling is a population-level asymptotic quantity computed with the evaluator's finite pools as the population; it is a diagnostic, not a bound on a finite draw. (5) The truth for real pools is the pool mean, with draws from the pool with replacement, so the evaluation checks inference for that finite population rather than for the original scientific population. (6) The evaluator's diagnostics for the candidate are marked as unverified. (7) The selection rule makes the interval data-dependent and the 1\\% margin has no theoretical guarantee. (8) Stratified PPI is implemented but has no reported metrics and is not compared numerically.\n222:\\paragraph{Experiments that would test the hypotheses.} The mechanisms above can be tested with controlled comparisons that we did not run: (i) PPI++ with the weight clip removed, to separate the clipping component from nonlinear calibration; (ii) the recalibrated interval with the fallback disabled and with a fixed choice of recalibrator, to separate selection effects from the second control variate; (iii) the Student-$t$ quantile replaced by the normal quantile; (iv) coverage and width of every baseline, computed on the same draws and reported per family; and (v) a grid over $n$ and $N$ on the real pools, where the theory predicts a gain that shrinks with $n$ (estimation cost of order $p/n$) and with $r$. Until these are available, the decomposition in Section~\\ref{sec:headroomcheck} should be read as a diagnostic of what is possible, not as an attribution of what the evaluated interval does.\n227:With labels, unlabeled data and predictor held fixed, the evaluated configuration (cross-fitted recalibration as a second control variate, Student-$t$ quantile and a PPI++ fallback) gave mean intervals 2.2\\% narrower than PPI++ on average in a sealed evaluation, with reductions between 0.2\\% and 4.0\\% by family, and 6.8\\% and 30\\% narrower than PPI with $\\lambda{=}1$ and the labeled-only interval. No component was ablated, so the gain is not attributed to recalibration. A closed-form population-level headroom diagnostic is small (aggregate 0.0129) and regime-dependent, and matches the observed margin closely only for the synthetic mixture and roughly for census income; it is zero for the plankton pool and does not account for the observed gain in four of five families. Coverage point estimates are 0.934 pooled and 0.91 for the lowest family, below the nominal 0.95, although the evaluator's tests did not reject. We read the result as conditional support for a small gain, not as evidence that PPI++ intervals can be shortened for free. The most useful next experiments are a coverage and width comparison of an unclipped PPI++ weight and each recalibration component in isolation, per-episode width and coverage reporting for all baselines, and a sweep over $n$.\n242:Settings of the evaluated interval: $K=5$ shuffled folds seeded from the candidate's random generator; isotonic fit with clipped out-of-bounds prediction; binned fit with $\\min(10,\\max(2,\\lfloor 0.8n/25\\rfloor))$ bins (10 for $n=500$, 6 for $n=200$); pooled standardization of features; ridge $10^{-6}\\,\\mathrm{tr}(A)/p$ on the weight system; Student-$t$ quantile via a Cornish--Fisher expansion with degrees of freedom floored at 3; PPI++ weight clipped to $[0,1]$; fallback margin 0.99. The evaluator enforces a 3\\,s per-estimate deadline on the evaluated estimator.\n244:\\paragraph{Contrast variants.} The label-free basis variant uses a continuous piecewise-linear basis of $\\Yhat$ with knots at pooled quantiles, the model size $p\\in\\{1,2,3,5,8\\}$ is chosen by leave-one-out PRESS with a 2\\% improvement margin, the standard error uses leverage-corrected leave-one-out residuals, and a guard keeps the width ratio against PPI++ above 0.85. The stacked variant fits seven calibrators (isotonic, three binned with 3, 5 and 8 bins, monotone and local-linear kernels, linear), combines them by non-negative least squares with 4 inner folds, repeats $K=5$ cross-fitting $R=5$ times under a time budget, and uses averaged-residual variance plus a split-dispersion term with $n-3$ degrees of freedom.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
